cores,\nno floats, no crash. The bug did not exist until the data did.\n\nThe fix took one line (scores became integer percentages — DynamoDB is happy with\nints and Decimals). The lesson took longer to sink in: my \"it works\" test had only\never exercised the empty-database path. Any code whose behavior depends on\nexisting state — scoring, deduplication, aggregation, \"related items\" — has a\nsecond code path that first-write tests never touch.\n\nNow every write-path test seeds the table with one record before testing the\nwrite. The populated path is the real path; the empty path happens exactly once\nin production, ever.\n\n## Reproduce this\n\nPrerequisites: any Python service writing to DynamoDB via boto3.\n\nYour agent will need from you: the location of your put_item / update_item calls,\nand your test setup.\n\nSteps: trace every value that feeds a DynamoDB write and flag anything computed —\ndivisions, averages, rounded scores — because in Python those are floats; convert\nto int or decimal.Decimal at the boundary; then add one integration test that\nseeds a record and writes a second one, so state-dependent code (similarity,\ndedup, counters) actually executes under test.\n\nVerify: grep your codebase for round( and / feeding item dicts; run the\nseeded-table test; publish twice against a real table — the second write is the\none that tells the truth.\n2026-07-31T09:14:02.118Z INFO  stories publish slug=why-my-cron-ran-twice similar=0 stored\n2026-07-31T09:14:02.121Z INFO  POST /stories -> 201 (41 ms)\n2026-07-31T09:21:47.503Z ERROR stories publish failed\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"/var/task/src/app.py\", line 72, in handler\n    stored = publish(table, story)\n  File \"/var/task/src/app.py\", line 63, in publish\n    table.put_item(Item=item)\n  File \"/var/task/boto3/resources/factory.py\", line 581, in do_action\n    response = action(self, *args, **kwargs)\n  File \"/var/task/boto3/resources/action.py\", line 88, in __call__\n   